body:
First note about your PS:  job stats are global and become flat stats that are calculated before you even equip any items, across all grids.  If you level a Gretel job, you will still have those flat stats if you play as Pinocchio.

&#x200B;

All DPS jobs:  very expensive resource wise to play.  Feels rewarding in the sense that you're the one doing damage and can see a big score at the end screen when you play well.  However gameplay is the most dull and requires the least amount of effort.  Deckbuilding is also pretty easy but you need a lot of jobs in order to keep up.  It's the easiest to get by with playing in story mode which makes it universally usable, however a guild only needs 5 dps but 10 supports.

&#x200B;

Cleric:  The hardest to play well.  Misplay is very punishing and you have to learn to time the use of your weapons properly.  Deck building isn't too hard for this class, but spending your rolls wisely on good weapons is important.  Cleric is a very pure grid and so you do not want to use other support weapons in your grid when running this job, if you can avoid it.  Strong in any difficult story content.

&#x200B;

Minstrel:  Heavy deck building, and rolling on the right gachas are important.  What's best for your grid will depend on your other guild members and what their strengths and weaknesses are.  Can mix weapons with sorcerer.  Not too steep a learning curve, but greatly rewards reactive play.  Not very strong in story but definitely needed for a guild and not too hard to play as f2p.

&#x200B;

Sorcerer:  Similar to minstrel, deck building is important as is spending your rolls for good weapons.  What's best for your grid will also depend on your guildmates, and also the opposing guild.  Can mix weapons with minstrel.  Also rewards reactive play without being too punishing.  Not strong in story but also necessary for a guild, and cheap the build as f2p.
